  <body class="px-page px-lp">

    <a href="#main" class="px-skip">Skip to content</a>

    <%- include("partials/nav", { pxFonts: false }) %>

    <main id="main">

      <!-- ═══ 1 · HERO — the thesis, and one sample of the behaviour ═══ -->
      <section class="px-hero">
        <div class="px-container">
          <h1>The AI that knows where your project stands.</h1>
          <p class="px-hero-sub">
            Tell Aqua about your startup once &mdash; what you&rsquo;re building, what you
            decided, what changed. It carries the current version into every conversation,
            shows where each fact came from, and lets you correct or delete any of it.
          </p>
          <div class="px-hero-ctas">
            <a href="/signup" class="px-btn px-btn-primary px-btn-lg">Get started</a>
            <a href="/pricing" class="px-link">See pricing</a>
          </div>

          <div class="px-artifact">
            <p class="px-voice">
              Welcome back. You moved the launch to May 2 last Tuesday &mdash; and the pricing
              page is still the blocker you mentioned.
            </p>
            <%# The example used to cite changed files and a teammate's thread —
                integrations Aqua does not have. It may only show what the welcome
                line can produce from what the user said or uploaded. %>
            <p class="px-voice-note">An example of how Aqua opens. It writes this only from what you told it or uploaded &mdash; it does not read your email, chat or repos.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- The throughline runs the length of the narrative: one hairline,
           one light pass in the reader's direction of travel, three rests. -->
      <div class="px-story">
        <div class="px-thread" aria-hidden="true"><div class="px-thread-light"></div></div>

        <!-- ═══ 2 · THE DAILY TAX ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec">
          <div class="px-container">
            <h2 class="px-tax-lead px-reveal">Every conversation starts from nothing.<br>So you explain it again.</h2>
            <ul class="px-tax-list px-reveal">
              <li class="px-tax-row"><span>the company</span><span class="px-again">again</span></li>
              <li class="px-tax-row"><span>the codebase</span><span class="px-again">again</span></li>
              <li class="px-tax-row"><span>what you already decided</span><span class="px-again">again</span></li>
              <li class="px-tax-row"><span>what you already tried</span><span class="px-again">again</span></li>
              <li class="px-tax-row"><span>who Priya is</span><span class="px-again">again</span></li>
            </ul>
            <p class="px-tax-close px-reveal">Then you close the tab. Tomorrow it starts from nothing.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 3 · WHAT IT COSTS ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec is-tight-bottom">
          <div class="px-container px-cost px-reveal">
            <h2>It isn&rsquo;t the minutes.</h2>
            <p>It&rsquo;s the questions you stop asking, because explaining the background costs more than the answer is worth.</p>
            <p>It&rsquo;s the work you do alone, because bringing anyone up to speed takes longer than doing it yourself.</p>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 4 · THE TURN ═══ -->
        <section class="px-turn px-beat">
          <div class="px-container">
            <h2>Aqua starts where you left off.</h2>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 5 · WHAT HAPPENS BEFORE IT ANSWERS ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec">
          <div class="px-container">
            <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
              <h2>What happens before it answers.</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="px-mech">
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>Everything you give it lands in one place.</h3>
                <p>Conversations, documents and code resolve to the same people, the same projects and the same threads. What you said in a conversation can support &mdash; or contradict &mdash; what a document claims.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>It decides what it needs to know.</h3>
                <p>Not what matches your question. What understanding the question requires: what you are working on now, what you decided before, what is connected to it, and how sure it is of each.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>Then it updates what it believes.</h3>
                <p>Every exchange changes something. A fact corroborated. A name connected. An assumption you corrected. Something that quietly stopped being true.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 6 · WHAT IT UNDERSTANDS ABOUT YOU ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec">
          <div class="px-container">
            <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
              <h2>What it understands about you.</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="px-world">
              <div class="px-world-item px-reveal">
                <div>
                  <p class="px-world-label">Your files</p>
                  <p class="px-voice">The pricing deck from March lists four tiers. The one you uploaded on Tuesday lists three.</p>
                </div>
                <p>It reads what you upload the way an analyst would &mdash; and it notices when two of your own documents disagree.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-world-item px-reveal">
                <div>
                  <p class="px-world-label">Your projects</p>
                  <p class="px-voice">Nothing calls <code>formatInvoice()</code> except the billing route you are editing.</p>
                </div>
                <p>It knows the shape of your codebase, not just its text: what depends on what, and what breaks if you change it.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-world-item px-reveal">
                <div>
                  <p class="px-world-label">What it remembers</p>
                  <p class="px-voice">You corrected this twice. It has held since April.</p>
                </div>
                <p>Every fact it keeps shows where it came from, how sure it is, and how many times you have revised it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 7 · IT ONLY CLAIMS WHAT IT CAN SHOW ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec">
          <div class="px-container">
            <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
              <h2>It only claims what it can show you.</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="px-promise">
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>It shows you why it thinks something.</h3>
                <p>Every fact it holds carries the message it came from. You can read the evidence, not only the conclusion.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>It won&rsquo;t guess about you.</h3>
                <p>It will not claim a pattern in how you work until it has seen it happen more than once &mdash; and it tells you how sure it is.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>Being wrong is recoverable.</h3>
                <p>When something you told it stops being true, the old version is set aside rather than deleted. Corrections keep their history.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <h3>You can see all of it, and delete all of it.</h3>
                <p>Everything it knows about you sits on one page. So does the button that removes it.</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 8 · IT GETS BETTER BECAUSE YOU USE IT ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec px-beat">
          <div class="px-container">
            <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
              <h2>It gets better because you use it.</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="px-grow">
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <p class="px-grow-when">Day one</p>
                <p class="px-voice">What are you working on?</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <p class="px-grow-when">Month one</p>
                <p class="px-voice">You left off on the auth migration.</p>
              </div>
              <div class="px-reveal">
                <p class="px-grow-when">Month six</p>
                <p class="px-voice">This is the third time billing has broken during a migration. Last time you fixed it by moving the backfill behind a flag.</p>
              </div>
              <p class="px-tax-close px-reveal">Nothing is assumed on day one. Everything after that is earned.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 9 · QUESTIONS ═══ -->
        <section class="px-story-sec">
          <div class="px-container">
            <div class="px-sec-head px-reveal">
              <h2>Questions.</h2>
            </div>
            <div class="px-faq px-reveal">
              <details>
                <summary>What does Aqua actually learn from?</summary>
                <p>The conversations you have with it, and the files you give it. Both carry the same standing: something you said in chat can support or contradict something a document claims &mdash; and either way you can see which message or which file it came from.</p>
              </details>
              <details>
                <summary>What if it gets something wrong about me?</summary>
                <p>Correct it in conversation. The old version is set aside rather than deleted, so a fact carries its history &mdash; including how many times you have revised it.</p>
              </details>
              <details>
                <summary>Can I see everything it knows about me?</summary>
                <p>Yes. Everything sits on one page, grouped and written as sentences rather than raw records. Each fact shows where it came from, when it was learned and how often it has been used. You can pin one, forget one, or forget all of it.</p>
              </details>

              <%# BLOCKED - TRAINING QUESTION. Do not ship without this.
                  A page that asks people to hand over their files and their
                  conversations must answer "is my work used to train models?"
                  in plain language; its absence is louder than its presence.
                  Awaiting the true answer - nothing is guessed here. Restore
                  by pasting a <details> block in this position, and add the
                  matching Question to the FAQPage graph above. %>

              <details>
                <summary>What happens on day one, before it knows anything?</summary>
                <p>It asks what you are working on, and it says nothing it cannot support. Nothing is assumed on day one. Everything after that is earned.</p>
              </details>
              <details>
                <summary>What does it cost?</summary>
                <p>Free credits every day, no card required. Prepaid packs are there when you need more, and purchased credits do not expire. <a href="/pricing" class="px-link">See pricing</a>.</p>
              </details>
            </div>
          </div>
        </section>

        <!-- ═══ 10 · CLOSE ═══ -->
        <section class="px-close px-beat">
          <div class="px-container px-reveal">
            <h2>Start where you left off.</h2>
            <p>Free every day. No card required.</p>
            <a href="/signup" class="px-btn px-btn-primary px-btn-lg">Get started</a>
          </div>
        </section>

      </div><!-- /.px-story -->
    </main>

    <%- include("partials/footer") %>

    <script>
      /* Motion 2 and 3 of the four this page allows. The hidden state lives
         under html.js (set in the nav partial before anything paints), so a
         visitor whose JavaScript never runs gets the complete page rather
         than a blank one. */
      (function () {
        var els = document.querySelectorAll('.px-reveal');
        if (!('IntersectionObserver' in window)) {
          for (var i = 0; i < els.length; i++) els[i].classList.add('in');
          return;
        }
        var io = new IntersectionObserver(function (entries) {
          entries.forEach(function (e) {
            if (e.isIntersecting) { e.target.classList.add('in'); io.unobserve(e.target); }
          });
        }, { threshold: 0.12, rootMargin: '0px 0px -8% 0px' });
        for (var j = 0; j < els.length; j++) io.observe(els[j]);
      })();
    </script>
